<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of LLM performance across seven QA tasks. WGM: Windowed Greedy Merging algorithm with window size <math id="Sx4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> = 64, and maximum group size <math id="Sx4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> = 32. Optimal results are in bold; suboptimal results are indicated by parentheses.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Baseline.</h4>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments on three related quantization methods to compare them with full precision (FP) and our WGM-LLM. The primary baselines are BiLLM and PB-LLM, which represent state-of-the-art (SOTA) models for binary post-training quantization (PTQ). We also perform experiments with GPTQ, the SOTA for 4-bit PTQ, further demonstrating the efficacy of WGM-LLM under extreme binarization. AG-BiLLM is excluded due to unavailability of the source code. PTQ-1.61 is similarly omitted because its implementation lacks support for contemporary LLM architectures, and the comprehensive updates required prohibitive development effort beyond the scope of this study.</p>
</div>
